>I hate the idea of finding a bag of holding, only to put an
>unidentified wand into it and have it blow up because the wand was a
>wand of cancellation. So I have a surefire (?) way to identify wands
>of cancellation.
>
>First, as with all wands (in my opinion), engrave something with your
>finger and then engrave with the wand. Depending on the message, you
>can identify the wand from the spoilers and then #name it. However,
>the messsage "The engraving on the floor vanishes!" could be one of
>three wands: cancellation, invisibility, or teleportation. So I #name
>this wand cancel-invis-tel. Then, go find the nearest (preferably
>slow) monster (not on a no-teleport level) and zap it with the wand.
>
>If the monster vanishes, the wand is either teleportation or
>invisibility. Attack the spot the monster was in. If there's nothing
>there (and it's a slow monster, non-moving monsters work best) then
>it's teleportation, re-#name the wand accordingly. If you hit
>something invisible, then the wand is invisibility. re-#name it
>accordingly.
>
>If the monster doesn't vanish, one of two things is going on. Either
>the wand is cancellation, or it's invisible but you can see invisible.

Or it has run out of charges.

>So attack the monster. If it says, "you attack the <monster>," then
>the wand is cancellation, if it says, "you attack the invisible
><monster>," then the wand is invisibility. re-#name accordingly.
>
>Then the next wand you find which gives you "The engraving on the
>floor vanishes!" can only be one of two wands. Repeat the above. Then
>the last of the three wands self-identifies after engraving with it.
>You'll never blow up a BoH again!

I really should try to employ this method (or similar) more often.
What I normally end up with is a bunch of wands called Canc/Tel/Invis
that I typically get around to identifying with ?oID at some point.

And I so rarely survive long enough to acquire a BoH that I have
